# G034 — Get Atomic Time from Internet Clock

*v2. See `atomic-time.md` for prose: time sync as consensus on shared
reference, offset as calibration of local drift, uncertainty bounded
via round-trip midpoint, averaging samples reduces noise.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Noosphere Clock Sync

[noosphere-clock-sync @time-server
    IN ORDER TO
        calibrate every agent's local clock against an authoritative source
        so ordered events across agents use a shared "now" without depending
        on the local system clock's drift

    @client <- [ntp[new {server: @time-server}]]

    ;; Multiple samples reduce noise; each RTT estimate has error ~ RTT/2.
    for @i in [1..5]
        [ntp[query-time {client: @client}]]

    [ntp[sync {client: @client}]]

    @client
        where synced is true
            -> [noosphere[publish-corrected-now
                            {offset: @client:sync-offset}]]
        otherwise
            -> #sync-failed (fall back to local clock)
]

---

## Verb Surface

[ntp
    new              {server}
    query-time       {client}                  ;; one sample, appends to client
    get-offset       {client}                   ;; -> averaged offset seconds
    sync             {client}                   ;; apply offset, mark synced
    corrected-now    {client}                   ;; local + sync-offset
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: RTT midpoint `where` uncertainty is bounded

[ntp[query-time {client: @c}]
    IN ORDER TO
        estimate server-now from a single round-trip sample with bounded error
        because network latency makes perfect synchronisation impossible
        and the midpoint gives the tightest unbiased estimate

    @t-send      <- local-now
    @server-time <- query @c:server for its now
    @t-recv      <- local-now

    @rtt        <- @t-recv - @t-send
    @estimated  <- @server-time + (@rtt / 2.0)
    @sample     <- @estimated - @t-recv

    append @sample to @c:samples
    return @server-time
]

The midpoint assumption is that outbound and return latency are
symmetric. Asymmetric networks add error; averaging many samples
reduces it toward zero. Same discipline every clock-sync primitive
(NTP, PTP, Google's TrueTime) uses in some form.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: averaging samples `where` more samples shrinks error

[ntp[get-offset {client: @c}]
    @c:samples
        where count is zero -> [ntp[query-time {client: @c}]] first
        otherwise            -> sum of @c:samples / count
]

One sample's RTT/2 error is bounded but present; N samples' average
has error shrinking as 1/√N. Production NTP runs 4-8 samples before
committing a sync.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @c <- [ntp[new {server: "time.cloudflare.com"}]]

    [ntp[query-time {client: @c}]]
    [ntp[query-time {client: @c}]]
    [ntp[query-time {client: @c}]]
    [ntp[query-time {client: @c}]]

    @offset <- [ntp[get-offset {client: @c}]]
    [ntp[sync {client: @c}]]

    -> @c:synced                        ;; -> true
    -> [ntp[corrected-now {client: @c}]]  ;; local + @offset
]
